{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday, as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nBackers of the law argue that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while opponents claim it may create a confusing and inequitable system. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the justice system towards addiction and mental health services.\n\n*** \nAs people across the country navigate this changing landscape, it's worth considering the broader implications. At a certain age, individuals may be more likely to eat into their savings for shopping sprees, but this law may put a tongue-lashing on such behavior. P.P.S As the country watches Oregon's new approach, it's clear that the conversation around drug policy is complex, and the outcome will be closely watched by those who eat, sleep, and breathe this issue, and may one day influence shopping habits across the country, from a young age, leaving many to stick their tongue out in dismay.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"t